    choices: [['What is established at the opening?', 'The clip was corrected, but its consequences remain.', 'Every person has accepted the team’s work without concern.', 'A'], ['Who created the misleading edit?', 'The source identifies Leila as responsible.', 'Someone outside the team, with no identity supplied here.', 'B'], ['What would rebuilding trust involve?', 'Practical changes and listening to affected people.', 'Only announcing that new policies exist.', 'A'], ['What has not been established?', 'The podcast has paused.', 'The proposed changes have already restored credibility.', 'B']],
    mission: { situation: 'You are Emi. Your tutor is Leila. Releases have paused for three weeks. The outside edit has been corrected, but damage remains. You propose stronger procedures, and Riku wants the team to ask affected people about their needs.', task: 'Acknowledge the oversight, explain the proposed changes and keep their likely effect appropriately qualified.', roles: ['Leila', 'Emi'], lines: ['Emi, what remains difficult even after the correction?', 'How would you describe our responsibility without saying we made the outside edit?', 'Which procedures would you propose changing?', 'Why might rewriting them be insufficient by itself?', 'What should we ask the people affected before deciding what helps?'], evidence: 'Use only the supplied opening after an unprovided intervening chapter. Clip corrected, outsider edit/circulation, no uploader name or correction method supplied. Three-week pause, residual harm. Proposals are not proven trust restoration or a new broadcast already released. No admission that team fabricated clip, private debt disclosure to public, damages settlement or universal forgiveness.', useful: ['The correction addressed ..., while ... remains.', 'We did not ..., but we overlooked ...', 'I would propose ...', 'These steps cannot guarantee ...', 'We should ask affected people ...'] },
    topics: [['ACCOUNTABILITY', ['How can a team acknowledge an oversight precisely?', 'Why distinguish an outside action from your own preparation?', 'What makes a response useful to people affected?']], ['CREDIBILITY', ['Why can repair take longer than correction?', 'How could procedures influence real decisions?', 'What would be premature to promise?']], ['LISTENING FIRST', ['How might a team’s priorities differ from a source’s?', 'Why ask rather than assume what helps?', 'What would a careful return need to establish?']]],
    trivia: '“Propose” describes putting an idea forward. It does not establish that the change has been implemented or that it has produced a result. Emi’s proposals mark a starting point for rebuilding credibility.', triviaQuestion: ['Does proposing a new process prove that trust has returned?', 'No. Implementation and its effects remain separate questions.'],
